1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,704
This session follows the reasoning, stroke and seizure sections of
Seven C Part B. The school asks you to connect mechanisms to what you
can assess and do now.

2
00:00:10,704 --> 00:00:20,184
Stroke and seizures can overlap, and both have important mimics. One
plausible label must not close the assessment of a changing patient.

3
00:00:20,184 --> 00:00:29,688
We will practise glucose-first, last-known-well timing, both FAST and
VAN, continuing seizures and postictal reassessment using original
examples.

4
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:40,584
Keep the school teaching and checked current clinical references
identified. Medication knowledge does not erase the actual PCP, ACP or
specialist boundaries.

5
00:00:40,584 --> 00:00:50,520
Normal includes the expected physiology and the person usual function.
A chronic deficit must be distinguished from a new one without
dismissing either.

6
00:00:50,520 --> 00:01:00,648
Gather current cues and compare them with the baseline. A vital sign
within a population range does not prove that compensation or serious
disease is absent.

7
00:01:00,648 --> 00:01:09,408
Interpret what the change may mean and consider a dangerous
alternative. Then choose the permitted action that addresses the
immediate risk.

8
00:01:09,408 --> 00:01:20,256
Reassess continuously and change the working explanation when evidence
changes. The five-step loop is a reasoning aid, not a guarantee that
the first pattern is right.

9
00:01:20,256 --> 00:01:30,216
Neurons require continuing energy supply for signaling and membrane
stability. Oxygen and glucose must reach the tissue through effective
blood flow.

10
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:41,112
Perfusion is more than a cuff pressure. Low output, vascular
obstruction or impaired oxygen carriage can threaten function through
different mechanisms.

11
00:01:41,112 --> 00:01:53,904
Autoregulation helps maintain cerebral flow over a limited range, but
acute disease can impair it. Do not aggressively lower pressure from a
generic adult chart during an undifferentiated stroke.

12
00:01:53,904 --> 00:02:05,592
Focal interruption can produce regional deficits; global hypoperfusion
can cause loss of consciousness. The clinical assessment must consider
both patterns and their overlap.

13
00:02:05,592 --> 00:02:16,200
When effective supply falls, aerobic energy production becomes
inadequate. Anaerobic metabolism cannot fully maintain the energy
needed for normal neuronal function.

14
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:26,280
Energy-dependent ion pumps become ineffective. Sodium and water shift
into cells while potassium distribution and membrane potential are
disturbed.

15
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:37,176
The resulting cellular swelling is cytotoxic edema. It is not the same
as saying every visible new weakness proves the patient has reached
one exact microscopic stage.

16
00:02:37,176 --> 00:02:48,120
The practical lesson is to preserve effective oxygenation and
perfusion and minimize avoidable delay to definitive stroke care, not
to infer a cell count from the examination.

17
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:58,272
Loss of membrane control can promote excessive excitatory signaling.
Glutamate receptor activation is part of the school ischemic-cascade
explanation.

18
00:02:58,272 --> 00:03:10,248
Abnormal calcium loading activates enzymes and pathways that can
damage membranes, proteins and genetic material. Oxidative stress adds
another injury mechanism.

19
00:03:10,248 --> 00:03:22,920
These events overlap rather than waiting for a perfect sequential
timetable. Necrotic and programmed cell-death processes do not appear
as uniquely identifiable stages on a field scale.

20
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:32,544
Understanding the mechanism explains urgency and secondary-injury
prevention. It does not create a field neuroprotective-drug or
reperfusion order.

21
00:03:32,544 --> 00:03:41,352
The core concept describes severely injured tissue, while penumbra
describes threatened tissue that may recover with timely effective
treatment.

22
00:03:41,352 --> 00:03:52,560
Collateral flow and individual conditions influence the rate of
progression. The school neuron-loss number is a model estimate, not a
bedside countdown for every patient.

23
00:03:52,560 --> 00:04:03,816
A field examination cannot measure core size or prove that all
recovery opportunities have closed. Current guidance includes benefit
for some selected larger-core patients.

24
00:04:03,816 --> 00:04:15,096
Document, support and mobilize promptly instead of denying urgency
because the symptoms seem old or stable. The definitive team
determines treatment eligibility.

25
00:04:15,096 --> 00:04:26,784
Regional brain dysfunction can affect movement, sensation, language,
vision or attention. Different vascular and network involvement
produces different combinations.

26
00:04:26,784 --> 00:04:38,016
Symptoms can fluctuate or resolve, but the risk still needs
evaluation. Do not require a continuous march from mild weakness to
coma before taking the event seriously.

27
00:04:38,016 --> 00:04:49,608
The textbook ischemic-self-limiting wording is not used as
reassurance. New deterioration can arise from continuing ischemia,
swelling or another systemic problem.

28
00:04:49,608 --> 00:05:00,288
Repeat focused findings and ABC observations with times. The change in
function is useful evidence even when the exact anatomical explanation
remains uncertain.

29
00:05:00,288 --> 00:05:08,160
Ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke require different definitive
treatments, but both can produce urgent neurological dysfunction.

30
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:20,256
Severe headache, vomiting, impaired consciousness or concerning
pressure/pupil findings raise concern for hemorrhage and
deterioration; they do not establish subtype by themselves.

31
00:05:20,256 --> 00:05:31,536
Do not automatically give aspirin or use a routine blood-pressure-
lowering plan for an undifferentiated new stroke. Follow the actual
pathway and preserve cerebral perfusion.

32
00:05:31,536 --> 00:05:42,168
A young patient can still have a stroke, and a seizure at the start
does not exclude it. Keep onset, glucose and focal findings attached
to the urgent assessment.

33
00:05:42,168 --> 00:05:51,960
The school uses CPSS, BE-FAST and FAST-VAN language. Learn which task
each serves rather than combining them into an invented score.

34
00:05:51,960 --> 00:06:04,896
Face, arm and speech screens identify common focal findings. Sudden
balance or eye symptoms can raise concern for presentations not
captured by a simple face-arm-speech result.

35
00:06:04,896 --> 00:06:16,296
Use the named local FAST-VAN form and document its individual
findings. The current provincial guidance asks for both FAST and VAN
regardless of the FAST result.

36
00:06:16,296 --> 00:06:26,808
A negative screen is not a rule-out, particularly when other sudden
neurological findings persist. Seek the indicated consultation and
destination decision.

37
00:06:26,808 --> 00:06:38,400
Obtain and document glucose early while immediate ABC support
continues. Do not delay ventilation or hemorrhage care to complete a
ceremonial glucose-first gate.

38
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:49,512
The checked adult E01 defines hypoglycemia below four millimoles per
liter. Use the permitted correction route with actual airway
protection and formulation checks.

39
00:06:49,512 --> 00:06:59,592
Do not give oral glucose to someone unable to protect the airway or
safely swallow. Recheck the measured value and the neurological
examination after treatment.

40
00:06:59,592 --> 00:07:12,504
Persistent focal deficits require continuing stroke assessment;
hypoglycemia and stroke can coexist. The provincial card image uses at
least four, not an invented higher threshold.

41
00:07:12,504 --> 00:07:23,784
Last known well is the last reliable time the person was at their
neurological baseline. Ask what the witness actually observed rather
than accepting the word normal without detail.

42
00:07:23,784 --> 00:07:32,040
Witnessed onset can be precise, but discovery may occur much later.
Record them separately when they are not the same event.

43
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:40,032
Include the date and source, and preserve uncertainty rather than
selecting a convenient time that creates eligibility.

44
00:07:40,032 --> 00:07:50,712
Obtain a reliable contact when possible so the receiving team can
verify the history. Do not postpone necessary transport while
searching for perfect certainty.

45
00:07:50,712 --> 00:08:00,264
Assume a fifty-nine-year-old had a reliable baseline conversation at
twenty-two ten the previous evening. That is the available last-known-
well reference.

46
00:08:00,264 --> 00:08:11,352
At six twenty in the morning, new weakness and language difficulty are
discovered on waking. Six twenty is discovery, not proof that the
stroke began then.

47
00:08:11,352 --> 00:08:24,216
The crew assesses at six thirty-seven. Record the dates, witness
source and unknown actual onset; do not substitute arrival, dispatch
or discovery for last known well.

48
00:08:24,216 --> 00:08:36,312
Wake-up and selected unknown-onset patients can still merit urgent
stroke treatment evaluation. Use the current local pathway, not an old
absolute two-hour destination rule.

49
00:08:36,312 --> 00:08:47,904
Ask for a smile and observe asymmetry, considering the usual baseline
and any facial injury. Describe the side and finding rather than just
writing abnormal.

50
00:08:47,904 --> 00:09:01,704
Use the trained arm instruction and observe drift or weakness. If
neither arm moves, reassess comprehension, global weakness,
consciousness and other limitations instead of automatically calling
it normal.

51
00:09:01,704 --> 00:09:13,320
Assess speech using an understandable task, distinguishing wrong words
or inability to name from slurred articulation. Language and hearing
barriers must be considered.

52
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:23,544
Attach symptom timing and baseline information to the findings. A
positive recognition screen is not a definitive vessel or stroke-
subtype diagnosis.

53
00:09:23,544 --> 00:09:33,096
The current local card emphasizes a preference to look to one side.
Describe the actual gaze finding and whether the patient can respond
to the assessment.

54
00:09:33,096 --> 00:09:44,640
The school supplement also discusses visual-field loss and double
vision. These matter clinically, but do not silently rename every
visual complaint as the formal card finding.

55
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:56,256
Check usual vision, eye disease, communication and the overall
neurological pattern. A new sudden visual deficit deserves attention
even if the face-arm-speech screen is negative.

56
00:09:56,256 --> 00:10:07,152
A positive clue raises LVO concern; it does not identify an occluded
artery or replace imaging. Document the finding and use the actual
triage pathway.

57
00:10:07,152 --> 00:10:18,432
Use familiar objects such as a pen or watch through the actual
assessment method. A naming problem is different from being unable to
see or recognize an unfamiliar object.

58
00:10:18,432 --> 00:10:27,072
Consider comprehension as well as expression. Someone may understand a
question yet be unable to produce the intended words.

59
00:10:27,072 --> 00:10:37,368
Dysarthria affects articulation and can coexist with aphasia. Record
the actual behavior rather than placing every speech change in one
category.

60
00:10:37,368 --> 00:10:47,616
Arrange accessible communication and consider baseline hearing or
language. Aphasia alone does not establish lack of thought or
decision-making capacity.

61
00:10:47,616 --> 00:10:56,952
Explain the task and ask the person to identify left, right or both
when touched, using the trained method and accessible instructions.

62
00:10:56,952 --> 00:11:07,296
Check each side separately before simultaneous stimulation. A primary
sensory deficit or failure to understand the task can complicate
interpretation.

63
00:11:07,296 --> 00:11:18,912
If a side is detected alone but ignored when both are stimulated,
describe that extinction or inattention finding. The local card
illustrates the commonly missed left side.

64
00:11:18,912 --> 00:11:29,832
Do not reduce neglect to weak muscles or insist no other side can be
affected. Record the actual result and limitations within the whole
neurological pattern.

65
00:11:29,832 --> 00:11:39,744
The current provincial guide asks for both FAST and VAN regardless of
the first result. Do not insert a weakness-only gate from a different
tool version.

66
00:11:39,744 --> 00:11:50,376
Both positive findings raise possible LVO concern and may influence
the regional destination. One positive screen still needs the
appropriate stroke-centre pathway.

67
00:11:50,376 --> 00:12:02,640
If both are negative but sudden posterior or other stroke concern
persists, the provincial pathway requires consultation for a stroke-
centre transport decision. Negative does not mean no disease.

68
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:11,784
Screening does not overrule an unsafe airway or unstable circulation.
The next destination slide keeps that exception visible.

69
00:12:11,784 --> 00:12:37,632
For an original exercise, a patient develops new facial weakness
involving the eyebrow, eye closure and mouth. That can fit a
peripheral pattern, but a brainstem lesion can resemble it. Ask about
onset and last known well, and assess associated speech, limb, vision
and coordination changes. Do not label the patient cleared of stroke
from the forehead finding alone.

70
00:12:37,632 --> 00:13:00,528
Apply the current local examination and transport pathway. Negative
FAST-VAN does not exclude stroke, and an uncertain onset after sleep
is not proof of slow development. Keep the history sources and
unknowns explicit. Do not test swallowing by giving water or make an
unsteady patient walk merely to complete an examination.

71
00:13:00,528 --> 00:13:14,040
The provincial adult pathway sends unstable airway or hemodynamic
cases to the nearest emergency department. Do not bypass needed
stabilization simply to reach a desired label of hospital.

72
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:25,560
For stable patients, use the closest and most appropriate stroke-
centre decision through the actual regional pathway. Local transport
times and available capability matter.

73
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:35,328
Possible LVO can favor EVT access where the pathway supports it, but
not every positive score automatically bypasses every thrombolysis
site.

74
00:13:35,328 --> 00:13:50,064
The cited provincial framework is adult, eighteen and older; suspected
pediatric stroke requires the indicated consultation. Current hospital
capability must be confirmed rather than assumed from an old textbook
list.

75
00:13:50,064 --> 00:14:02,952
Support the actual airway, breathing and circulation needs. The
checked local stroke oxygen wording is saturation at least ninety-four
percent, not automatic maximum oxygen forever.

76
00:14:02,952 --> 00:14:12,744
Check glucose and correct hypoglycemia safely, then recheck function.
Do not delay mobilization to prove every possible mimic in the home.

77
00:14:12,744 --> 00:14:26,400
The saved F03 prefers an eighteen-gauge site above the wrist, while
F00 says at least twenty gauge without transport delay. Preserve that
difference and follow the actual pathway.

78
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:39,696
Aim to minimize scene time; the guide twenty-minute goal is not
permission to use twenty minutes unnecessarily. Obtain a reliable
witness contact and perform nonurgent tasks en route when appropriate.

79
00:14:39,696 --> 00:14:50,928
Notify the receiving facility as early as practical while mobilizing
through the pathway. Use the approved channel for required identity
information, not a public teaching example.

80
00:14:50,928 --> 00:15:02,832
Give the reliable onset or last-known-well time, discovery and wake-up
context, including uncertainty. Do not collapse several different
events into one invented stroke time.

81
00:15:02,832 --> 00:15:15,264
Report individual FAST-VAN and other relevant findings, current
vitals, glucose, correction and response. Include anticoagulant
information and the source when known.

82
00:15:15,264 --> 00:15:27,144
Finish with ETA, current support and the capability needed. A pre-
alert prepares the team; the fuller handover still preserves the
detailed record and witness contact.

83
00:15:27,144 --> 00:15:38,016
A fifty-seven-year-old at a sewing studio has new weakness and
difficulty naming familiar objects. Glucose measures three point zero,
with a reliable method.

84
00:15:38,016 --> 00:15:49,080
Provide needed ABC support and correct confirmed hypoglycemia through
the authorized airway-safe route. Do not assume an oral route is safe
because the person is awake.

85
00:15:49,080 --> 00:16:00,768
On recheck, glucose is five point two, but the new naming problem and
unilateral arm findings persist. Corrected glucose does not close the
neurological assessment.

86
00:16:00,768 --> 00:16:12,888
Continue the stroke pathway, verify LKW and discovery, and communicate
both treatment and persistent findings. One mimic can coexist with the
time-critical condition.

87
00:16:12,888 --> 00:16:23,928
Normal networks balance excitatory and inhibitory influences. The
school uses glutamate and GABA to explain why a disruption can permit
abnormal activity.

88
00:16:23,928 --> 00:16:37,032
Genetic, structural, metabolic, toxic or infectious factors can
contribute. A known epilepsy diagnosis is only part of the history,
not an explanation of every event.

89
00:16:37,032 --> 00:16:47,928
Seizure manifestations can include sensory change, behavior, altered
responsiveness or movement. Not every seizure is a generalized
convulsion.

90
00:16:47,928 --> 00:16:58,032
Describe what actually occurred and maintain the current functional
assessment. Visible shaking does not remove the need to check
breathing, pulse and injury.

91
00:16:58,032 --> 00:17:11,424
The current ILAE framework retains focal, generalized, unknown whether
focal or generalized, and unclassified categories. Do not force
incomplete witness information into a precise diagnosis.

92
00:17:11,424 --> 00:17:24,216
The book older partial, simple and complex terms may appear in
examination reading. Recognize the terminology while describing
consciousness and the actual sequence in the current encounter.

93
00:17:24,216 --> 00:17:35,736
A focal symptom can be followed by bilateral convulsions, but not
every event must begin as one visible focal lesion. Unknown onset
should remain unknown.

94
00:17:35,736 --> 00:17:46,632
Functional or dissociative seizures are not an epileptic generalized
subtype. Neither witnesses nor psychiatric history proves that
diagnosis at the scene.

95
00:17:46,632 --> 00:17:57,360
Tonic-clonic activity may include stiffening followed by repeated
jerking, with altered consciousness. Not every event shows every older
textbook stage.

96
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:06,936
A reported aura may itself be a focal seizure symptom. Ask what the
person felt rather than assuming all convulsions have an obligatory
warning.

97
00:18:06,936 --> 00:18:19,608
Record the actual sides, movements, start/stop times, responsiveness
and breathing. A useful description helps more than an overconfident
classification from a brief glimpse.

98
00:18:19,608 --> 00:18:29,784
If the patient is unresponsive with abnormal breathing, consider
arrest as well as seizure. Some collapse events have brief movements
from poor cerebral perfusion.

99
00:18:29,784 --> 00:18:39,600
For continuing tonic-clonic activity, five minutes is the operational
early threshold, and repeated seizures without recovery are also
urgent.

100
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:52,056
The ILAE distinguishes this from a later estimated risk of long-term
injury. Do not wait until twenty or thirty minutes, and do not claim
that every neuron crosses an exact injury cliff at five.

101
00:18:52,056 --> 00:19:02,808
Ask when the event actually started and whether recovery occurred. If
the start is unknown or activity continues on arrival, do not reset
the clock to zero.

102
00:19:02,808 --> 00:19:14,232
Provide necessary ABC support and arrange timely advanced help and
conveyance. The actual seizure-control intervention must match the
operator and protocol.

103
00:19:14,232 --> 00:19:26,472
Sustained neural and muscle activity raises metabolic demand. Early
autonomic responses can include fast pulse and high pressure, but
these do not guarantee adequate perfusion.

104
00:19:26,472 --> 00:19:38,064
Oxygen and glucose demands, impaired breathing and heat production can
increase systemic stress. Monitor the actual function rather than a
fixed compensation-phase timer.

105
00:19:38,064 --> 00:19:51,192
Ventilation or circulation can fail, and prolonged muscle injury can
add potassium and kidney complications. These are risks to anticipate,
not diagnoses established by dark urine alone.

106
00:19:51,192 --> 00:20:02,208
Treat current threats and escalate early. Anticipation is useful when
it improves support and communication, not when it delays necessary
care for more testing.

107
00:20:02,208 --> 00:20:12,000
Protect the patient from surrounding hazards and cushion appropriately
without pinning down convulsive movement. Consider injury and maintain
dignity.

108
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:23,136
Do not force the mouth open or insert objects or fingers blindly. An
airway adjunct requires its actual indication and a safe opportunity,
not brute force.

109
00:20:23,136 --> 00:20:34,080
Use positioning and suction as safely indicated, with effective airway
and breathing support. Do not create a second injury by forcing a
rigid body onto its side.

110
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:45,960
The old textbook thirty-second apnea wait is not used. Credible
inadequate breathing needs prompt support and skilled escalation, not
a mandatory period of neglect.

111
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:57,720
Check measured glucose and oxygenation/ventilation while care
proceeds. Metabolic derangement can cause a seizure and can also
complicate another neurological event.

112
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:07,272
Consider trauma, stroke, infection and other new neurological disease.
A previous epilepsy diagnosis does not make a different event routine.

113
00:21:07,272 --> 00:21:19,224
Ask about prescribed medicines, access problems, recent changes,
alcohol/drugs and any rescue dose already given. Use neutral questions
rather than blame.

114
00:21:19,224 --> 00:21:31,944
In pregnancy or a relevant postpartum context, consider eclampsia and
the obstetric pathway. Magnesium and advanced anticonvulsants require
the actual role-specific authorization.

115
00:21:31,944 --> 00:21:47,232
The school saved body lists IM or IV midazolam under a PCP heading.
The checked current F02 places seizure-control midazolam at ACP, so
that shortcut is not reproduced as ordinary PCP permission.

116
00:21:47,232 --> 00:22:00,744
PCP care includes effective support, confirmed hypoglycemia treatment
and the indicated airway/access tasks. Knowing advanced drug
mechanisms helps coordination without crossing the boundary.

117
00:22:00,744 --> 00:22:11,352
Record rescue medicines already given by family or carers, including
route, dose and time. They affect the next dose and respiratory-risk
assessment.

118
00:22:11,352 --> 00:22:23,832
F02 advises against prophylactic benzodiazepines when the patient is
not currently seizing. Persistent reduced consciousness still needs
assessment, not automatic sedation.

119
00:22:23,832 --> 00:22:34,632
Recovery can include confusion, fatigue or reduced responsiveness,
with a variable duration. Continue airway, breathing and circulation
assessment.

120
00:22:34,632 --> 00:22:46,704
Recheck glucose, injury, perfusion and focal findings. Hypoxia, drugs,
stroke or continuing nonconvulsive activity can resemble a prolonged
postictal state.

121
00:22:46,704 --> 00:22:58,488
Persistent or worsening dysfunction needs escalation, not a promise to
wait an arbitrary number of hours. EEG-dependent diagnoses cannot be
proved from a screen alone.

122
00:22:58,488 --> 00:23:10,752
Provide quiet respectful explanation and reorientation, without
assuming behavior is deliberate misconduct. Calm engagement and
medical reassessment can occur together.

123
00:23:10,752 --> 00:23:21,696
A focal deficit after a seizure may be postictal, but a stroke can
also provoke a seizure. Do not declare Todd-type weakness without
maintaining the time-critical differential.

124
00:23:21,696 --> 00:23:30,792
Corrected glucose does not exclude coexisting stroke. Reassess the
same functional tasks and record what resolved and what remained.

125
00:23:30,792 --> 00:23:41,592
A functional-seizure or psychiatric history does not prove the cause
today. Respect the person and assess the actual neurological and
physiological changes.

126
00:23:41,592 --> 00:23:51,984
Resolved or fluctuating deficits still require evaluation and the
applicable pathway. Do not convert normal appearance at arrival into
no event occurred.

127
00:23:51,984 --> 00:24:44,352
The historical table names anti-amyloid treatments aducanumab/Aduhelm
and lecanemab/Leqembi separately from symptom medicines. An old row is
not proof of present availability or Canadian eligibility. The
selected current lecanemab label warns about amyloid-related imaging
abnormalities: ARIA-E involves edema and ARIA-H hemorrhagic findings.
Some patients have no symptoms, while serious or fatal events can
occur; focal deficits can mimic ischemic stroke. In a new deficit,
support the patient, preserve last-known-well time and urgently report
the exact anti-amyloid treatment and timing. Do not diagnose ARIA or
cancel the stroke pathway from a drug name; the receiving team needs
imaging and treatment-specific decisions.

128
00:24:44,352 --> 00:25:37,848
The table also names brexpiprazole/Rexulti for Alzheimer-associated
agitation and suvorexant/Belsomra for insomnia, not cognition recovery
or immediate rescue. The checked U.S. brexpiprazole label is not an
as-needed agitation treatment and retains increased-mortality warnings
in older adults with dementia-related psychosis; its Alzheimer-
agitation indication is not permission for every psychosis. Suvorexant
can cause somnolence, impaired alertness/coordination and complex
sleep behaviors, with particular respiratory-function concerns. A
quieter or sleepier patient is not necessarily better. Continue
respectful de-escalation and assess breathing, glucose, perfusion and
the new neurological change; do not add or stop these medicines from a
study slide.

129
00:25:37,848 --> 00:25:50,280
Syncope is transient loss of consciousness from global cerebral
hypoperfusion. Arrhythmia, volume loss and other causes can create
serious risk even after rapid recovery.

130
00:25:50,280 --> 00:26:01,464
Brief jerking or incontinence does not prove an epileptic seizure.
Conversely, an event in a standing person is not automatically simple
vasovagal syncope.

131
00:26:01,464 --> 00:26:13,680
Assess breathing, pulse, glucose, relevant rhythm, trauma and
symptoms. Do not stand an unstable or injured person merely to obtain
orthostatic observations.

132
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:25,032
Describe the witness sequence and recovery and use the actual pathway.
Age, position or a quick return to conversation cannot independently
exclude dangerous disease.

133
00:26:25,032 --> 00:26:36,000
A forty-two-year-old at a florist workshop is still convulsing when
the crew arrives. A witness reports several minutes and no clear
recovery between repeated episodes.

134
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:48,072
Protect from hazards, assess airway/breathing and circulation, check
glucose when safe and provide needed support. Do not force the mouth
open or restrain movement.

135
00:26:48,072 --> 00:26:59,328
Clarify the actual start and any rescue medicine while arranging the
appropriate advanced response and conveyance. Do not start a fresh
five-minute wait from crew contact.

136
00:26:59,328 --> 00:27:11,688
Report continuing/recurrent activity, lack of recovery, current
physiological threats and actual prior doses. The next team needs a
timeline, not merely the word seizure.

137
00:27:11,688 --> 00:27:24,720
For stroke, preserve LKW, witnessed onset and discovery with dates and
sources. For seizures, preserve activity and recovery intervals and
whether the clock is uncertain.

138
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:36,144
Give individual findings and their change, including side, language
task, consciousness, glucose and ABC state. A scale total alone loses
useful information.

139
00:27:36,144 --> 00:27:46,152
Record actual treatment and response, including family-administered
medicines and adverse effects. Do not record a considered action as
completed.

140
00:27:46,152 --> 00:27:58,896
State uncertainty and what capability is needed next. Appropriate non-
conveyance requires the real criteria and consultation, not a blanket
assumption that recovery equals safety.

141
00:27:58,896 --> 00:28:53,784
The literal headache page adds a focused question: is this a familiar
pattern or a new, abruptly severe or changing headache? Its vascular,
tension and organic categories organize history, not a field
diagnosis. Perform the general and neurological assessment, ask about
time, fever, trauma, pregnancy/postpartum state and exposures, and
seek urgent evaluation for a concerning change. F04 names
acetaminophen first-line and says not to give ASA for headache. Other
analgesics, antiemetics, oxygen or fluid support depend on the real
indication, exclusions and operator level. A migraine history or
symptom relief does not exclude hemorrhage or another serious cause; a
general reference to nitrous oxide does not override its
contraindications.

142
00:28:53,784 --> 00:29:45,552
Original cross-check: nine days after birth, a person with a migraine
history develops a different severe headache and visual disturbance;
reliable pressure is 168 over 112. The history is not clearance to
label another migraine. Repeat a valid measurement while primary care
and urgent assessment proceed, check neurological function and
glucose, establish the postpartum timeline and report the severe
pressure and new symptoms. Consider obstetric and neurological threats
together rather than waiting for rash, proteinuria or a complete
textbook triad. Seizure prophylaxis and antihypertensive treatment
require their actual level and consultation; the learner job is timely
recognition, permitted support and a truthful handover, not
independent advanced prescribing.

143
00:29:45,552 --> 00:29:55,368
You should now explain the school cerebral-perfusion and seizure
mechanisms without pretending that a visible symptom reveals a
guaranteed cellular stage.

144
00:29:55,368 --> 00:30:04,728
Perform the reasoning behind glucose-first, both FAST and VAN,
reliable LKW and the stability-dependent destination decision.

145
00:30:04,728 --> 00:30:14,016
Recognize continuing/recurrent seizures and support the real ABC
threats while coordinating the correctly authorized next intervention.

146
00:30:14,016 --> 00:30:27,552
Use the bilingual Nancy reading and original cases to teach the
sequence back. The next school sections develop pregnancy, aortocaval
compression, toxic patterns, kidney injury and GI threats.
